# Digital Scoundrel

You are an AI pair-programmer working on a faithful digital adaptation of the solo card game **Scoundrel**.
Primary goal: implement the core game loop with minimal UI (text + simple card visuals).
Secondary goal: polish UI, assets, accessibility & interactions.

## 0 Non-Negotiables

- Faithful rules implementation comes before polish.
- Prefer deterministic, testable game logic.
- Separate:
  - Pure game state transitions (engine)
  - Randomness (deck shuffle / draw)
  - UI rendering & input
- Every rule should map to:
  - a state field
  - a transition function
  - a validation function
  - tests

## 1 Game Overview (Core Loop)

### 1.1 Premise

The player explores a dungeon represented by a standard deck of cards. The player’s goal is to survive the entire deck by managing health, equipment, and room-by-room decisions.

### 1.2 Core Interaction

At any point the player is in exactly one of:

- Starting a run (initialize, shuffle, deal)
- In a room (4 face-up cards available to choose from, resolving choices)
- Between rooms (cleanup / draw next room)
- Run ended (win/lose + score)

### 1.3 Win / Lose

- Win: player survives until the Dungeon deck is exhausted **and** the final remaining room cards are resolved.
- Lose: player HP reaches 0 (or below) at any time.

Acceptance:

- End conditions are deterministic and unit-tested.

## 2 Components & Terminology

### 2.1 Scoundrel Deck (“Dungeon”)

Start with a standard 52-card deck.

Remove (not used in the game):

- All Jokers (if present in the physical deck)
- All **Red face cards**: J♥, Q♥, K♥, J♦, Q♦, K♦ (6 cards)
- All **Red aces**: A♥, A♦ (2 cards)

This leaves (from a 52-card deck):

- Monsters (♠/♣): A-K = 26 cards (includes black aces and face cards)
- Potions (♥): 2-10 = 9 cards
- Weapons (♦): 2-10 = 9 cards

Total: 44 cards (plus “remove Jokers” note for physical decks).

### 2.2 Card Types (by suit)

- Spades: Monsters
- Clubs: Monsters
- Hearts: Health Potions
- Diamonds: Weapons

### 2.3 Ordered Values (Monsters)

Monsters (clubs/spades) deal damage equal to their **ordered value**:

- 2–10 = pip value
- J = 11
- Q = 12
- K = 13
- A = 14

Weapons and potions only exist as ranks 2–10, and their value is their pip value.

Acceptance:

- One canonical “monster ordered value” mapping used everywhere.
- Tests confirm monster mapping for A, J, Q, K and 2–10.

### 2.4 “Room”

A Room is the set of **up to 4 face-up cards** in front of the player.

- At the start of a turn, reveal cards until there are **4 cards face-up** in the Room, if possible.
- At the end of a normal turn, **exactly 1 card remains** face-up and carries forward into the next Room.

(When the Dungeon is low/empty, the Room can be fewer than 4.)

## 3 Player State

### 3.1 Health

- `hp`: integer
- `hpMax`: integer

Start values (default, unless overridden in config):

- `hpMax = 20`
- `hp = hpMax`

Healing:

- HP cannot exceed `hpMax`.

Acceptance:

- Healing clamps.
- Damage reduces hp.
- Game ends on hp <= 0.

### 3.2 Weapon (Equipment)

Player may hold at most one weapon.

- `weapon`: null | { value: int, lastSlainMonsterValue: int|null, slainStack: Card[] }

Rules:

- Weapons (♦ 2–10) are **binding**: if you take one, you **must equip it**.
- Equipping a new weapon **replaces** the old weapon.
- When replacing a weapon, the **old weapon and any monsters stacked on it** go to the discard pile.

Acceptance:

- Exactly one weapon at a time.
- Replacing weapon discards the old weapon _and_ its stacked monsters.

### 3.3 Weapon Usage Constraint (“Descending Monster Rule”)

When a weapon is used to slay a monster, that monster is placed on the weapon’s stack.

Constraint:

- After a weapon has slain a monster of value **X**, that weapon may only be used to slay monsters of value **<= X** until the weapon is replaced.

Implementation:

- `weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue`:
  - `null` initially (weapon unused) → weapon can be used on any monster.
  - Set to the slain monster’s value each time the weapon is used.
- The constraint applies **only when choosing to fight using the weapon**.
- Fighting barehanded does not change `weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue`.

Acceptance:

- If `weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue = X`, weapon-fighting is only allowed for monsters with value `<= X`.
- After a weapon fight, `weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue` updates to that monster’s value.

## 4 Setup / Initialization

### 4.1 Start Run

- Create the Scoundrel deck (see section 2.1).
- Shuffle (seedable RNG).
- Initialize player:
  - hp = 20
  - hpMax = 20
  - weapon = null
- Initialize turn flags:
  - `previousTurnAvoided = false`
- Reveal cards until Room has up to 4 cards.

Acceptance:

- Given a fixed RNG seed, the entire run is reproducible.
- Initial Room is correct size based on remaining deck.

## 5 Turn Structure (Room Resolution)

Each turn starts with a Room of up to 4 face-up cards.
If fewer than 4 are face-up and the Dungeon has cards, reveal more until 4 or the Dungeon is empty.

On a turn, the player either:

- **Avoids the Room** (special action), or
- Resolves **exactly 3 of the 4 cards** (or as many as exist if fewer than 4), one-by-one.

### 5.1 Avoiding the Room (“Avoid Room”)

The player may avoid the Room:

- Take all face-up Room cards in one action and place them at the **bottom of the Dungeon** (preserving their order relative to each other or as a single block; choose one convention and test it).
- Then immediately reveal cards until Room has up to 4 cards for the next turn.

Restriction:

- You may avoid as many Rooms as you want, but you may **not avoid two Rooms in a row**.

Implementation:

- Track `previousTurnAvoided: boolean`.
- Avoid is allowed only if `previousTurnAvoided == false` **and** the Room has 4 cards available (because the rule is “scoop up all four cards”).

Acceptance:

- Avoid moves all 4 Room cards to the bottom of the Dungeon.
- Avoid is blocked if the last turn was also an avoid.
- Avoid does not change HP/weapon.

### 5.2 Resolving Cards (Normal Turn)

If the player does not avoid:

- The player must resolve **3 of the Room’s cards**, one at a time.
- After resolving 3 cards, the turn ends and the **4th card remains face-up** to become part of the next Room.

If the Dungeon is low/empty and fewer than 4 cards exist in the Room:

- The player resolves as many as required until the Dungeon is exhausted and the Room is empty (this may take multiple turns with fewer than 4 cards available).

Turn-limited rule:

- The player may use **at most one Health Potion per turn**.

Implementation:

- Track `potionUsedThisTurn: boolean` reset at the start of each normal (non-avoid) turn.

Acceptance:

- A normal turn resolves exactly 3 cards when 4 are available.
- Exactly 1 card carries forward to the next Room after a normal turn.
- Potion usage is limited to one per turn.

## 6 Card Resolution Rules

### 6.1 Monster (spades/clubs)

When a Monster is chosen, player chooses one of:

1. **Fight with weapon** (only if a weapon is equipped and the weapon usage constraint allows)
2. **Fight barehanded**

Effects:

- Barehanded:
  - Damage = monsterValue
  - Monster is discarded
- With weapon:
  - Damage = max(0, monsterValue - weapon.value)
  - Monster is added to `weapon.slainStack`
  - `weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue = monsterValue`
  - Monster is not discarded separately (it lives on the weapon until the weapon is replaced; at replacement, it is discarded with the weapon)

Acceptance:

- Weapon fight is blocked if `weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue != null` and `monsterValue > weapon.lastSlainMonsterValue`.
- Damage calculation is correct and clamped at 0.

### 6.2 Health Potion (hearts 2–10)

- If `potionUsedThisTurn == false`:
  - hp = min(hpMax, hp + potionValue)
  - potion is discarded
  - set `potionUsedThisTurn = true`
- If `potionUsedThisTurn == true`:
  - potion is discarded
  - hp does not change

Acceptance:

- Healing clamps to hpMax.
- Second potion in the same turn provides no healing.

### 6.3 Weapon (diamonds 2–10)

- The player must equip it.
- If a previous weapon exists:
  - Discard the previous weapon **and** all monsters in its `slainStack`.
- New weapon starts with:
  - `value = weaponValue`
  - `lastSlainMonsterValue = null`
  - `slainStack = []`

Acceptance:

- Equipping replaces.
- Old weapon + its stack are discarded together.

## 7 Room Transition

### 7.1 After Avoid

- Room becomes empty (because all 4 were moved to bottom of Dungeon).
- Set `previousTurnAvoided = true`.
- Next turn: reveal until Room has up to 4 cards.

### 7.2 After Normal Turn (3 cards resolved)

- Exactly 1 Room card remains face-up.
- Set `previousTurnAvoided = false`.
- Next turn: reveal up to 3 new cards to bring Room to 4 (or as many as the Dungeon has).

### 7.3 End of Game Handling

- The game ends immediately when `hp <= 0` (loss).
- The game ends in a win when:
  - The Dungeon is empty **and**
  - The Room is empty (all remaining cards have been resolved).

Acceptance:

- Carry-over behavior works correctly.
- Correct handling when the Dungeon has fewer than 3 cards remaining to refill the Room.
- Victory triggers only after Dungeon and Room are both empty.

## 8 Scoring

Compute score at the end of the run:

- If the player **loses** (hp <= 0):
  - Find all remaining **monsters** still in the Dungeon (unseen/undrawn).
  - Score = `hp - sum(remainingMonsterValues)` (a negative number in typical play; preserve exact arithmetic).
- If the player **wins** (Dungeon empty and Room empty):
  - Score = `hp`, **except**:
    - If `hp == 20` and the **last resolved card** was a Health Potion, score is `hp + potionValue` (i.e., 20 + that potion’s value).

Acceptance:

- Loss score subtracts remaining monsters in Dungeon only.
- Win score matches rule, including the special “last card potion at full health” case.

## 9 Data Model (Implementation Guidance)

### 9.1 Canonical Types

- Card:
  - id: unique string (stable)
  - suit: "spades"|"clubs"|"hearts"|"diamonds"
  - rank: "A"|"2"|...|"10"|"J"|"Q"|"K"
  - type: "monster"|"potion"|"weapon" (derived from suit)
  - value:
    - if monster: ordered value (A=14, J=11, Q=12, K=13, 2–10=pip)
    - else: pip value (2–10 only)

- GameState:
  - rngSeed: number|string
  - deck: Card[] (Dungeon; choose one convention for “top” and document)
  - discardCount: int
  - room:
    - cards: Card[] (face-up)
  - player:
    - hp: int
    - hpMax: int
    - weapon: null | { value: int, lastSlainMonsterValue: int|null, slainStack: Card[] }
  - turn:
    - potionUsedThisTurn: boolean
    - previousTurnAvoided: boolean
    - lastResolvedCard: null | Card (for scoring)
  - status: "init"|"in_room"|"won"|"lost"
  - score: null | int

Notes:

- Discard pile is **face-down** in the physical game. Digitally, track count for UI; keep identities hidden by default.

### 9.2 Transition Functions (Pure)

Implement pure reducers like:

- startRun(seed, config) -> GameState
- revealToRoomSize(state) -> GameState
- resolveCard(state, cardId, choice) -> GameState
- avoidRoom(state) -> GameState
- endRunAndScore(state) -> GameState

Acceptance:

- No mutation of input state (copy-on-write).
- All transitions validate preconditions and return either:
  - newState
  - or an error object (preferred for UI messaging)

## 10 Minimal UI Requirements (v1)

- Display:
  - HP (current/max)
  - Equipped weapon value (or none)
  - Weapon constraint indicator (e.g., “weapon can only slay <= X” when `lastSlainMonsterValue` is set)
  - Room cards (face-up): show suit + rank + derived type + value
  - Remaining Dungeon count
  - Discard count (only count; do not reveal discard identities)
  - Whether avoiding is currently allowed (blocked if last turn avoided)

- Inputs:
  - Click/select a card
  - If monster: choose “weapon fight” (if allowed) or “barehanded”
  - If potion/weapon: single resolve action
  - Avoid Room action (only when allowed)

Acceptance:

- Player can complete a full run via UI without console commands.
- Invalid actions are blocked with a clear message.

## 11 Config (Do Not Overbuild)

Provide a single config object:

- hpMax (default 20)
- roomSize (default 4)
- rulesetName (string)

Acceptance:

- Defaults implement the base game.
- Config is injected into engine, not hard-coded in UI.

## 12 Testing Requirements

### 12.1 Unit Tests (Engine)

Must cover:

- Deck creation (52 unique cards) and removal rules (red aces, red face cards; jokers not present in a 52-card deck)
- Monster ordered value mapping (A=14, J/Q/K, 2–10)
- Room reveal logic (fill to 4 when possible)
- Turn structure: resolve 3, carry 1
- Potion clamp + one-per-turn rule
- Weapon replace discards old weapon + its stack
- Monster damage formula (weapon and barehanded)
- Weapon usage constraint (must be <= last slain value)
- Avoid: moves all 4 room cards to bottom; cannot avoid twice in a row
- Win/lose conditions
- Scoring (loss subtract remaining monsters; win special potion-at-20 case)

### 12.2 Determinism Test

Given fixed seed:

- initial Room composition is identical across runs
- full run sequence is reproducible with same player choices

Acceptance:

- Tests run headless; no dependency on Love2D graphics.

## 13 Technical Notes (Love2D)

- Use a single source of truth: `GameState`.
- Love2D layer:
  - collects input
  - calls pure engine transitions
  - renders state
- Randomness:
  - Seed RNG at startRun
  - Do not call RNG anywhere else except shuffle/reveal utilities.

Acceptance:

- Engine can be executed in isolation (Lua module) without Love2D runtime.

## 14 Explicit Non-Goals (v1)

Do NOT implement yet:

- Meta progression / upgrades / floors
- New card types / modified deck
- Animations, particles, sound
- Save/load, unlocks
- Multiple characters or classes

Acceptance:

- Any PR adding v2 features must be rejected unless explicitly requested.

## 15 Open Items / Known Ambiguities

- When avoiding a Room, placing “all four cards at the bottom” is a single action; define and test whether that preserves their internal order as a block (recommended) or reverses/shuffles them (not recommended).
- Discard is face-down; keep identities hidden by default, but allow debug inspection in dev/test harness if needed (not player-facing).

Acceptance:

- Rules are centralized here; code follows this doc.
